# Python testing

Every new or changed Python module must get tests in `tests/` before the work is done. Use `pytest`. Never hit real services, databases, or files with real data.

## Fake data only

- Build fixtures with invented values (`"Jane Doe"`, `"note-0001"`, `"555-0100"`).
- Never use real patient names, clinical notes, credentials, API keys, or production-like records.
- Mock I/O: `unittest.mock.AsyncMock` / `MagicMock`, or pytest monkeypatch. Services under test must not call the network or a live DB.

```python
# ❌ BAD — real-looking clinical data, no mocks
def test_create_note(client):
    client.post("/notes", json={"patient": "Maria Silva", "cpf": "123.456.789-00"})

# ✅ GOOD — obviously fake payload, service mocked
def test_create_note_returns_schema(client, mocker):
    mocker.patch.object(NoteService, "create", return_value=NoteRead(
        id=1, title="fake-title", body="lorem-ipsum-not-real"
    ))
    response = client.post("/notes", json={"title": "fake-title", "body": "lorem-ipsum-not-real"})
    assert response.status_code == 201
    assert response.json()["title"] == "fake-title"
```

## Coverage expectations

- One test file per `route.py` / `service.py` (happy path + error/exception path).
- Assert status codes and response schema fields, not full snapshot dumps of internals.
- If a function cannot be tested with fakes, the design is wrong — inject dependencies instead of skipping tests.
